  <main class="index-page">
    <div class="index-hero">
      <h1>The World Tipiṭaka Project (1999–2024)</h1>
      <p>
        From the 40-Volume Roman-Script Transliteration (<em>Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500</em>)
        to the <em>Sajjhāya</em> Phonetic Recitation and Monotone Music Notation Transcription.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        Overview: Preserving the 2,500-Year Oral &amp; Textual Heritage
      </h2>
      <p>
        Initiated in Bangkok in B.E. 2542 (1999) by the <strong>M.L. Maniratana Bunnag Dhamma Society Fund</strong>
        (and continued by the <strong>World Tipiṭaka Foundation</strong>) under the supreme patronage
        of <strong>His Holiness Somdet Phra Ñāṇasaṁvara (Prince Krom Luang Vajirañāṇasaṁvara)</strong>,
        the 19th Supreme Patriarch of Thailand, the World Tipiṭaka Project was established upon
        international request to publish the complete Theravāda Pāḷi Tipiṭaka in standard Roman
        script and preserve its authentic phonetic recitation as a <em>Gift of Peace and Wisdom</em>
        to the world.
      </p>
      <p>
        As His Holiness the Supreme Patriarch recounted in his 1999 Benediction, before entering
        Parinibbāna the Buddha declared to Venerable Ānanda (<em>Mahāparinibbānasutta</em>,
        <a href="/tipitaka/7D/3.html#p_7D_484">7D:484</a>):
      </p>
      <blockquote
        style="margin: 1rem 0; padding: 0.85rem 1.25rem; border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); background-color: var(--badge-light-bg); border-radius: 0 0.375rem 0.375rem 0;"
      >
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.35rem; font-style: italic;" lang="pi">
          “…yo vo, ānanda, mayā dhammo ca vinayo ca desito paññatto, so vo mamaccayena satthā…”
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-secondary);">
          “Ānanda, the Dhamma (Doctrine) which I have expounded and the Vinaya (Discipline) which I
          have established for you all—that Dhamma and Vinaya will be your Teacher after I am gone.”
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        Spanning <strong>40 volumes</strong> (<strong>88 books</strong>, <strong>2,708,706 words</strong>,
        <strong>9,442,442 syllables</strong>, and <strong>20,606,104 letters</strong> in Roman script),
        the Tipiṭaka comprises three divisions recognized as the Heritage of Humanity:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
        <li>
          <strong>Vinayapiṭaka (5 volumes, Books 1V–5V):</strong> The Book on Monastic Discipline,
          forming the foundation of humanity’s oldest continuously practiced legal and procedural
          code.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Suttantapiṭaka (23 volumes, Books 6D–28Mi):</strong> The Book on the Discourses,
          preserving the Buddha’s dialogues and the <em>Vipassanā</em> methodology for training and
          liberating the mind.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Abhidhammapiṭaka (12 volumes, Books 29Dhs–40P24):</strong> The Book on Ultimate
          Realities, a systematic analytical repository of Dhamma terminology and supramundane
          phenomena of consciousness.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

