    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        Preparation of the Roman-Script Edition &amp; the Threefold International Standard
      </h2>
      <p>
        The authoritative textual baseline of the World Tipiṭaka Edition is the
        <strong>Chaṭṭhasaṅgīti (Sixth International Buddhist Council) Edition</strong> convened in
        Yangon in B.E. 2500 (1956–1957) by 2,500 Theravāda scholar-monks. Because His Holiness
        Somdet Phra Ñāṇasaṁvara had personally attended the Council as a representative Pāḷi scholar
        from Thailand, he designated the commemorative Roman-script edition as
        <strong>“Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500”</strong> (<em>The Buddhist Era 2500 Great
        International Council Pāḷi Tipiṭaka, Roman Script 2005</em>) and nominated over 500 patrons,
        advisors, and steering committee members to oversee its preparation.
      </p>
      <p>
        As noted by <strong>Venerable Phra Dhammapiṭaka (P. A. Payutto)</strong> in his 2002
        introduction, the working committee discovered discrepancies and printing anomalies across
        different printings of the original manuscripts. To achieve unprecedented textual fidelity:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong>Four Complete Canonical Recitations:</strong> Canonical Pāḷi experts recited the
          entire 40-volume text four times between B.E. 2543–2545 (2000–2002) and B.E. 2549–2550
          (2006–2007) to verify every Pāḷi sound and eliminate printing errors.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong>Collation Across 18 International Editions:</strong> Proofreaders cross-checked
          the manuscript against 18 modern Tipiṭaka editions in national scripts (comprising over
          2,000 volumes, later donated to and enshrined in the <em>International Tipiṭaka Hall</em>
          at Chulalongkorn University).
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong>100,000+ Hours of Dhamma Technology:</strong> A 50-member computer engineering
          team together with volunteers from Chulalongkorn University invested over 80,000 hours
          (2 years) creating the custom graphic layout and typesetting corpus, 20,000 hours (3 years)
          preparing the 2003–2005 printing, and 4 additional years (2005–2009) building the XML
          markup and the <strong>World Tipiṭaka Database</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
        This 12-year foundational effort established a <strong>Threefold International Standard</strong>:
      </p>
      <ol style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <li>
          <strong>Tipiṭaka Publication Standard:</strong> Open-source electronic typesetting in
          standardized Roman script and International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) characters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Electronic Tipiṭaka Open Standard:</strong> The structured Pāḷi Tipiṭaka Database
          and XML markup accessible to modern information technology worldwide.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tipiṭaka Reference Standard:</strong> The 40-volume <em>International Tipiṭaka
          Studies Reference</em> (including the critical apparatus notes, table of contents, index of
          verses, page concordances across 8 printed editions, and Pāḷi word index).
        </li>
      </ol>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        From Roman Script to the <em>Sajjhāya</em> Phonetic &amp; Monotone Music Notation Editions
      </h2>
      <p>
        Although the Roman alphabet made the Pāḷi Tipiṭaka readable worldwide, the project observed
        that readers unfamiliar with ancient Pāḷi grammar and syllabic segmentation struggled to
        pronounce the Roman-script text accurately, often introducing local tonal inflections or
        misreading syllable lengths.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beginning in 2010, linguists led by <strong>Ācāriya Siri Petchai</strong> (Pāḷi Pariyatti 9),
        <strong>Prof. Emeritus Dr. Vichin Panupong</strong>, <strong>Prof. Dr. Chidchanok Lursinsap</strong>,
        <strong>Brig. Gen. Suradhaj Bunnag</strong>, <strong>Prof. Duangjai Thewtong</strong>, and
        <strong>Assoc. Prof. Dr. Sasi Pongsarayuth</strong> turned to the clear phonetic orthography
        of King Rama V’s <strong>1893 Syām-Script Chulachomklao Edition</strong> (specifically its
        <em>Mai-A</em> short-vowel orthography) and cross-referenced every single one of the
        <strong>9,442,442 syllables</strong> of the Tipiṭaka against the ancient
        <strong>Kaccāyana Pāḷi Grammar</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Through this linguistic and computational “reverse engineering,” the project addressed four
        essential principles of canonical Pāḷi recitation (<em>Sajjhāya</em>):
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong>Rhythmic Precision of Short and Long Vowels:</strong> Ensuring uniform, synchronized
          chanting based strictly on vowel duration rather than arbitrary melodic tempo.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong><em>Lahu</em> (Quick) and <em>Garu</em> (Prolonged) Syllables:</strong> Explicitly
          marking 1-metre quick syllables (<em>Lahu</em>) and 2-metre heavy/prolonged syllables
          (<em>Garu</em>, including phonological shifts at verse-foot boundaries per
          <em>Kaccāyana</em> and <em>Vuttodaya</em> rules) to prevent orthographic corruption
          (<em>akkhara-vipatti</em>).
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong><em>Ṭhāna-Karaṇa</em> (Places &amp; Manners of Articulation):</strong> Preserving
          the exact oral articulation across all 41 Pāḷi sounds (from guttural to labial, aspirated
          vs. unaspirated) as codified in <em>Kaccāyana-Pāḷi</em> (<em>Akkharāpādayo ekacattālīsaṁ</em>).
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">
          <strong>Monotone Non-Tonal Recitation:</strong> In accordance with the <em>Vinayapiṭaka
          Cullavagga</em> prohibition against singing the Dhamma with drawn-out musical pitch
          (<em>āyatakena gītassarena</em>), Pāḷi is an Indo-Aryan language without lexical tone.
          <strong>Pāḷi Monotone Music Notation</strong> (comprising 95 custom symbols and
          <strong>225,964,457 digitally typeset symbols</strong>) was created in 2015–2016 to
          standardize <em>Lahu</em> and <em>Garu</em> rhythm without tonal distortion.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        Backed by <strong>World Tipiṭaka Patent No. 46390</strong> (2016) for automatic syllabic
        segmentation and AI-assisted audio compilation, the project produced the
        <strong>80-Volume Sajjhāya Phonetic Recitation Tipiṭaka</strong> alongside
        <strong>3,052 hours (1.6 TB)</strong> of digital recitation audio, and in 2020 expanded the
        electronic database into four parallel phonetic script notations:
        <strong>Pāḷi-Roman</strong>, <strong>Pāḷi-Tai</strong>,
        <strong>Pāḷi-Kirillitsa (Cyrillic)</strong>, and <strong>Pāḷi-Pinyin</strong>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        Global Presentations as a Royal Gift of Peace &amp; Wisdom
      </h2>
      <p>
        Following the 1893 precedent of her grandfather King Chulalongkorn (Rama V),
        <strong>Her Royal Highness Princess Galyani Vadhana Krom Luang Naradhiwas Rajanagarindra</strong>
        (Honorary President of the World Tipiṭaka Project since 2003, honored by UNESCO as an Eminent
        Personality of the World 2023–2024) led the inaugural worldwide presentations in 2005:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
          <strong>Sri Lanka (6 March 2005):</strong> Presented to the President of Sri Lanka on a
          royal pilgrimage to Colombo, enshrined at the Presidential Mansion in the land where the
          Pāḷi Tipiṭaka was first committed to writing.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
          <strong>Kingdom of Thailand (15 August 2005):</strong> Presented to the Thai nation and
          enshrined at the Constitutional Court of the Kingdom of Thailand in Bangkok.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
          <strong>Kingdom of Sweden (13 September 2005):</strong> Presented to Uppsala University as
          the inaugural western repository, preserved in the Carolina Rediviva Library alongside King
          Rama V’s 1893 Syām-Script edition.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          <strong>International Court of Justice, The Hague (12 October 2007):</strong> Presented at
          the Peace Palace and received by ICJ President Judge Rosalyn Higgins on the occasion of
          H.M. King Bhumibol Adulyadej’s 80th Birthday Anniversary, followed by presentations to over
          150 major libraries, courts, and universities across more than 30 countries.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

